luci-app-firewall: drop the dead TCP CCA option

The "TCP CCA" dropdown in Firewall -> Zone Settings -> General Settings is
a coolsnowwolf/luci downstream addition that was never wired to anything.
It writes firewall.@defaults[0].tcpcca and nothing reads that key: grep
finds no consumer in the lede package tree, in any feed, or in fw4.uc.
Upstream OpenWrt's luci-app-firewall has no such option at all.

Measured on the device: selecting "reno" and applying leaves
net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control at cubic -- which is simply the kernel
default (CONFIG_DEFAULT_TCP_CONG="cubic"). All three values are equally
inert; cubic only looks like it works because it matches that default.

The one component that really does apply a CCA from UCI is turboacc's init
script, and it reads turboacc.config.tcpcca out of /etc/config/turboacc --
a different file behind an identically named dropdown. It applies the value
with a plain sysctl and never touches the firewall config, which is exactly
why the firewall page's copy has no reader.

This holds in both firewall generations, and the patch is applied to both.
The dropdown is not gated on fw4: zones.js calls addTCPCCAOption() from
both branches of the fullcone test, so it renders under fw3 as well. On fw3
it is arguably worse, because turboacc ships in DEFAULT_PACKAGES.router and
its own working TCP CCA dropdown sits one page away -- two controls with the
same label, one of which does nothing.

Contrast fullcone on the same page, which is live in both generations and
stays: firewall3 parses it (FW3_OPT("fullcone", int, defaults, fullcone)),
firewall4 reads it via fw4.default_option("fullcone"), and turboacc writes
that same key rather than a private one. Wiring tcpcca up the same way would
mean teaching firewall4 to run a sysctl -- congestion control is not a
packet-filtering concern, and on fw4 kmod-tcp-bbr is not installed, so one
of the three offered values could not work anyway.

Besides being misleading, the option costs an "unknown option 'tcpcca'"
warning on every fw4 run (fw3 was not checked for the equivalent), and
rmempty=false means the key gets rewritten
into /etc/config/firewall every time the page is saved, so it cannot be
cleaned up from the device while the dropdown exists.

Removing it: the closure and both call sites go, leaving the fullcone
branch otherwise untouched. Congestion control belongs in sysctl, not in a
firewall UI -- see FriendlyWrt, which ships it commented out in
/etc/sysctl.d/31-optmize-proxy.conf and never touches luci-app-firewall.

Rebased onto 26d2f86ae2 ("luci-app-firewall: show runtime NAT and TCP CCA
status"), which put a read-only status line above the form and inserted
its setup code right in front of the closure -- the old hunk no longer
found its context and the build stopped. That commit displays /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_congestion_control
and leaves the dropdown as it was; it adds no reader for the uci key. With
both on the page the dead control contradicts the live value right above
it, so the status line stays and becomes the only CCA shown. History check:
a3ac471c96, which introduced the dropdown, never shipped a reader either,
and no later lede or luci commit touches tcpcca.

Proposed upstream as coolsnowwolf/luci#418, which also drops the two
zh_Hans strings; this patch leaves the .po alone to keep its footprint
small. Once that merges, this patch reports as already applied and can go.

